But wait — there's a subtle issue with the "can override built-in words" test: `: swap dup ;` then `1 swap` → [1,1]. The definition captures the built-in dup. No problem.

And "definitions are case-insensitive": `: SWAP DUP Dup dup ;` — the name is lowercased to "swap", and the tokens DUP/Dup/dup all compile to the built-in dup. Good.

One more consideration: the `:` token — what if a user defines a word named `:`? Not in the tests; ignore.

Another subtle point: what if a definition references a word defined later? Not in the tests; it would be an undefined error at compile time. That's fine.

Also, should we handle a `;` outside a definition? Not in the tests. It would compile as an undefined word and error. Fine.

Let's double-check the "execute in the right order" test: `: countup 1 2 3 ;` then `countup` → [1,2,3]. Good.

"can override built-in operators": `: + * ;` then `3 4 +` → 12. The definition of `+` captures the built-in `*`. Good.

